## 4. THE FAILURE

### 4.1 Gödel's Incompleteness Theorems

Gödel's theorems demonstrated the failure formally:

#### Theorem 1: Incompleteness
```
∀F (Consistent(F) ∧ Powerful(F) → ∃φ (φ ∉ Prove(F)))
```

Any consistent, sufficiently powerful formal system cannot prove all true statements.

#### Theorem 2: Unprovability of Consistency
```
∀F (Consistent(F) → ¬Prove(F, Consistent(F)))
```

Any consistent formal system cannot prove its own consistency.

### 4.2 The Implication

The implication is devastating:

| Gödel's Result | What It Means |
|----------------|---------------|
| Incompleteness | Logic cannot capture all truth |
| Unprovable consistency | Logic cannot justify itself |
| Self-reference | Logic cannot escape its own limits |
| Necessity of outside | Logic needs something beyond itself |

### 4.3 The Formal Proof of Insufficiency

The proof proceeds:

| Step | Statement | Justification |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | Logic is a formal system | Definition |
| 2 | All formal systems are incomplete | Gödel's Theorem |
| 3 | Logic cannot prove its own consistency | Gödel's Theorem |
| 4 | Logic cannot justify itself | From 2, 3 |
| 5 | Logic needs a ground outside itself | From 4 |
| 6 | Logic is not self-grounding | From 5 |

Therefore:

```
∀L (Logic(L) → Need(L, Ground) ∧ ¬SelfGround(L))
```

> **Logic proved it cannot be God.**
> **Logic proved it needs God.**

